From ed6844074f5744d97d28ab0859598c34f283d864 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <enrico.bartky@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 18:23:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] raster: replay the display list into surface tiles in
 parallel
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Multithreading roadmap item #5. The display list is already flat, immutable and
ordered; what was missing was a surface that can hand out independent views of
disjoint parts of itself, and a rasterizer that does not walk the pixels a clip
is certain to reject.

* BCanvas keeps a running intersection of its including clips and narrows every
  primitive's pixel loop to it. Exact — a pixel the box excludes is one the
  per-pixel test already rejected — and it is what lets a tile view draw its own
  rows instead of iterating the surface and discarding most of it.
* BCanvas.CreateTileView returns a canvas over the same surface carrying the
  current transform and clip, plus the tile as one further clip. The transform
  goes across unchanged, so a primitive's device coordinates are computed by the
  same arithmetic on the same inputs and the tiled image is identical rather
  than equivalent.
* GraphicsAdapter implements ITileParallelSurface and IBoundsCullingSurface. Its
  clip stack goes to a tile view UNNARROWED: DrawClippedImage re-derives a
  scaled image's source rectangle from the clip, so a tile-narrowed clip
  resampled the image and moved one row of pixels on two WPT tests.
* RGraphicsRasterBackend offers the replay to the tile driver, and only for a
  list whose every item the raster canvas draws on its own. It also culls an
  item whose drawn rectangle this file itself derives, which stops each tile
  paying for a gradient tile bitmap only one tile can see.
* The text backend rejects a run whose row band the clip cannot admit, from the
  font's own ascent and descent, before looking at a glyph.

Pixels identical at 1, 2, 3, 4 and 8 tiles across the corpus and the WPT run.
